WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.480
Identifying environmental-justice communities is both a data task and a duty of attention.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:16.809
A threshold can reveal a pattern, but it can also hide people whose burdens occur at a smaller scale, through a different indicator, or through conditions not represented in the tool.

3
00:00:16.809 --> 00:00:26.235
This independent study uses California DOJ's Best Practices for Implementing SB one thousand and current Government Code section sixty-five thousand three hundred two.

4
00:00:26.235 --> 00:00:28.944
It is educational preparation, not legal advice.

5
00:00:28.944 --> 00:00:34.973
The DOJ guide describes more than one route for identifying disadvantaged communities under current law.

6
00:00:34.973 --> 00:00:39.494
A real analysis must use the operative statute and authoritative current data.

7
00:00:39.494 --> 00:00:45.465
Do not collapse the routes into one score or assume that one screening tool decides the legal question.

8
00:00:45.465 --> 00:00:46.798
Use a layered baseline.

9
00:00:46.798 --> 00:00:49.465
First map pollution and environmental burdens.

10
00:00:49.465 --> 00:00:55.088
Second examine socioeconomic and health vulnerability using appropriate, privacy-protective data.

11
00:00:55.088 --> 00:01:01.812
Third gather local evidence about routes, timing, indoor conditions, cumulative stressors, and community boundaries.

12
00:01:01.812 --> 00:01:04.479
Fourth review planning and investment history.

13
00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:08.710
Document dataset date, geography, missing values, uncertainty, and scale.

14
00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:12.246
Census tracts are analytical units, not complete communities.

15
00:01:12.246 --> 00:01:16.420
Conditions near a road, facility, or parcel may differ within one tract.

16
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.111
Create a methods log before looking at results.

17
00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:26.098
State the statutory pathway being evaluated, data sources, thresholds, supplemental evidence, and who reviewed the method.

18
00:01:26.098 --> 00:01:28.331
Test sensitivity to reasonable changes.

19
00:01:28.331 --> 00:01:32.912
If the conclusion changes, report that instead of choosing the preferred output.

20
00:01:32.912 --> 00:01:35.489
Engage residents before boundaries are fixed.

21
00:01:35.489 --> 00:01:39.555
Ask how people name their community and which conditions the data miss.

22
00:01:39.555 --> 00:01:42.017
Protect individuals from re-identification.

23
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:43.703
Torrance application segment.

24
00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:50.390
This independent exercise cites the City of Torrance General Plan page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

25
00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:57.309
Prepare a hypothetical screening memo for the planning area using current authoritative data, but label it exploratory.

26
00:01:57.309 --> 00:02:00.973
Compare more than one geography and include a data-gap section.

27
00:02:00.973 --> 00:02:03.647
Invite correction through accessible outreach.

28
00:02:03.647 --> 00:02:09.927
Do not announce that Torrance has or lacks a legally defined disadvantaged community based on this exercise.

29
00:02:09.927 --> 00:02:17.079
Any real determination requires current law, complete local facts, the City's official process, and qualified legal advice.

30
00:02:17.079 --> 00:02:22.871
Good identification work makes burdens visible without pretending that people are points on a map.

31
00:02:22.871 --> 00:02:27.363
It combines lawful methods, transparent data, local knowledge, and humility.

32
00:02:27.363 --> 00:02:31.381
DOJ state-authored text is treated as public-domain source material.

33
00:02:31.381 --> 00:02:36.582
Third-party datasets, graphics, and local-government policy language are not reproduced.

34
00:02:36.582 --> 00:02:38.119
No endorsement is implied.
